  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Atlas Air has $565M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Atlas Air received 339 awards so far this fiscal year and the average award size is $1.7M. Obligations fell 25.6% year over year. Fiscal-year detail shows a big jump in FY25 with $759M and a smaller total in FY26 so far at $565M. Atlas Air is highly concentrated in one market: Nonscheduled Chartered Passenger Air Transportation accounts for $563M of obligations. Atlas Air also has obligations in Scheduled Freight Air Transportation of $1.1M, Other Technical and Trade Schools of $333K, and Flight Training of $332K. The current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is still in progress. Contractors, program managers, and procurement analysts tracking Atlas Air should note the concentration in passenger charter services and the year-over-year decline in obligations when evaluating competitive posture, contract renewals, and resource planning for the remainder of the fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why did Atlas Air obligations fall 25.6% year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 25.6% year over year. Atlas Air had $759M obligated in FY25 and has $565M obligated in FY26 to date. Atlas Air’s concentration in Nonscheduled Chartered Passenger Air Transportation, which accounts for $563M of FY26 obligations, means changes in that market have an outsized effect on the company’s federal totals."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Atlas Air’s federal revenues by market?",
        "body": "Atlas Air is highly concentrated in Nonscheduled Chartered Passenger Air Transportation with $563M in obligations. Atlas Air’s next largest market is Scheduled Freight Air Transportation with $1.1M. Atlas Air also has obligations in Other Technical and Trade Schools of $333K and in Flight Training of $332K."
      }
    ]
  },
